<Base title="Indexes" description="How a database index finds rows: sorted entries, B-tree searches, range scans, composite and covering indexes, when an index stops paying, and what each index costs every write.">
 <article class="visual-essay relational-design">
  <header class="essay-header">
   <p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage</p>
   <h1>Indexes</h1>
   <div class="essay-meta"><span>Relational deep dive</span><a href="/learn/databases/relational/">Relational introduction ↗</a><a href="/learn/databases/relational/query-execution/">Next: How a query runs →</a></div>
  </header>

  <p>An <strong>index</strong> is a separate, sorted copy of one or more columns of a table. Each entry holds a value from those columns and the location of the row it came from. Because the entries are sorted, every entry with the same value sits together in the index, even when the rows themselves are scattered through the table.</p>
  <p>That arrangement is what makes a lookup fast. Finding one customer’s 60 orders in a table of 60,000 means reading all 1,725 of the table’s pages when there is no index. With an index on the customer column, PostgreSQL read 2 index pages to find the 60 entries, then the 60 table pages they point to.</p>
  <p>The same arrangement sets the limits. An index helps most when a query constrains its first column. It hands rows back in its own order, which may or may not be the order the query wants. And every insert and update has to keep it sorted.</p>
  <p class="example-note">The figures use an invented table of 36 orders, six to a page, so every row stays visible. The measurements use a generated table of 60,000 orders, built by SQL you can run yourself.</p>

  <nav class="index-contents" aria-label="In this article"><ol><li><a href="#scan">Sequential scan</a></li><li><a href="#entries">Index entries</a></li><li><a href="#btree">B-tree</a></li><li><a href="#range">Range scans</a></li><li><a href="#selectivity">Selectivity</a></li><li><a href="#composite">Composite indexes</a></li><li><a href="#covering">Covering indexes and index-only scans</a></li><li><a href="#writes">Index maintenance</a></li><li><a href="#check">Checking an index with EXPLAIN</a></li></ol></nav>

  <section id="scan">
   <h2>Sequential scan</h2>
   <p>A <strong>sequential scan</strong> reads every page of a table in order and tests each row against the query’s conditions. It is what PostgreSQL does when no index fits the question.</p>
   <p>PostgreSQL stores a table in fixed-size <strong>pages</strong>, 8 kB each by default. The table’s main storage is called the <strong>heap</strong>, because rows sit in no particular order: a new row goes wherever there is room. Figure 1 scans the invented table for customer 42. Watch the counters: only four rows match, but the scan reads every page to be sure there are no others.</p>
   <ScanFigure number={1} />
   <p>The scan read 6 pages and checked 36 rows to return 4. Its cost follows the size of the table, not the size of the answer.</p>
   <p>The generated table shows the same thing at scale. Run the setup in a fresh PostgreSQL 18 session. It creates a temporary table of 60,000 invented orders for 1,000 customers, 60 orders each. Every row carries a long delivery note, so rows are wide and the table fills 1,725 pages.</p>
   <Sql code={setupText} label="Create and fill a temporary table of 60,000 generated orders" />
   <p>Now look for customer 42. <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> runs the query and reports what each step did and how many pages it touched. The <a href="#check">last section</a> reads these reports in detail.</p>
   <Sql code={explain(lookup)} label="Find customer 42’s orders and report the work" />
   <p>The plan is a <code>Seq Scan</code>. It read all 1,725 pages and discarded 59,940 rows to return 60.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="entries">
   <h2>Index entries</h2>
   <p>An <strong>index entry</strong> pairs a key with a row location. The <strong>key</strong> is the indexed column’s value. The location is the row’s physical address, which PostgreSQL calls a <strong>TID</strong> (tuple identifier): a page number and a position on that page. Order 3 in the invented table has TID (0,3), the third row on page 0.</p>
   <p>Figure 2 builds an index on <code>customer_id</code>. Watch the entries leave the table in table order and then sort. Customer 42’s rows are on four different pages, but their entries end up side by side.</p>
   <BuildFigure number={2} />
   <p>Once the entries are sorted, finding customer 42 means finding the first 42 and reading forward until the key changes. The table itself is not reordered. The index holds the order, and the TIDs lead back to the rows.</p>
   <p>Create the same index on the generated table and run the lookup again.</p>
   <Sql code={customerIndex} label="Create an index on customer_id" />
   <p>The plan now reads 2 index pages and 60 table pages instead of 1,725. Those 60 table pages are the remaining cost. Customer 42’s orders sit on 60 different pages, and each one needs a visit.</p>
   <p>PostgreSQL also avoids storing the same key 60 times. Since version 13, a B-tree index can store a repeated key once with a list of TIDs, called a <strong>posting list</strong>. In the generated index, customer 42 is one key with 60 TIDs. The figures draw one entry per row so that every pointer stays visible.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="btree">
   <h2>B-tree</h2>
   <p>A <strong>B-tree</strong> is a tree of index pages that lets a search jump to the right place in a sorted index instead of reading it from the start. It is PostgreSQL’s default index type, and what <code>CREATE INDEX</code> builds unless told otherwise.</p>
   <p>The sorted entries live on <strong>leaf pages</strong>. Above them, <strong>internal pages</strong> hold one key for each child page, marking where that child’s range begins. The single page at the top is the <strong>root</strong>. A search reads the root, follows the child whose range holds the key, and repeats until it reaches a leaf.</p>
   <p>Figure 3 draws the invented index as a B-tree lying on its side, with the root on the left. Pick a customer and watch the search read one page at each level.</p>
   <DescentFigure number={3} />
   <p>Every search in this tree reads three index pages, whatever the key: root, branch and leaf. That count is the tree’s <strong>depth</strong>, and it grows very slowly. A real page holds hundreds of entries, so each extra level multiplies the number of rows the tree can reach by hundreds.</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-labelledby="depth-caption"><table><caption id="depth-caption">B-tree depth for an index on one integer column, measured in PostgreSQL 18</caption><thead><tr><th>Rows</th><th>Levels</th><th>Index pages</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>100,000</td><td>2</td><td>276</td></tr><tr><td>1,000,000</td><td>3</td><td>2,745</td></tr><tr><td>10,000,000</td><td>3</td><td>27,422</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
   <p>A freshly built leaf page held 367 integer entries. Going from 1 million rows to 10 million added no level at all. A lookup for one key among the 10 million read 4 pages: 3 in the index and 1 in the table.</p>
   <p>The pages at each level are also linked to their neighbours on both sides. According to the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/btree.html#BTREE-STRUCTURE">PostgreSQL documentation</a>, more than 99% of a typical B-tree’s pages are leaves, so those links mostly connect leaf to leaf. The next section uses them.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="range">
   <h2>Range scans</h2>
   <p>A <strong>range scan</strong> descends to the first entry at or above a lower bound, then reads entries in order until it passes the upper bound. B-trees handle <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>=</code>, <code>&gt;=</code>, <code>&gt;</code> and <code>BETWEEN</code> this way. They can also serve <code>IN</code>, <code>IS NULL</code>, and <code>LIKE</code> patterns anchored at the start, such as <code>'abc%'</code>, though that last one depends on the column’s collation.</p>
   <p>Figure 4 runs a range scan for customers 20 to 34 on the invented index. Watch how many times the search descends, and what it does when it reaches the end of a leaf page.</p>
   <RangeFigure number={4} />
   <p>The search descended once, to the first 20. At the end of each leaf page it followed the link to the next leaf instead of going back to the root. It stopped at 42, the first key past the range. The entries came out in key order, so the <code>ORDER BY</code> needed no extra work.</p>
   <p>On the generated table, customers 40 to 45 have 360 orders between them.</p>
   <Sql code={range} label="Read the orders for customers 40 to 45 in customer order" />
   <p>The index part of the plan read 3 pages, the root and two leaves, to collect all 360 entries. The table part read 70 pages. The index holds the entries in order, but the plan still sorted the rows.</p>
   <p>The sort comes from how PostgreSQL chose to visit the table. It used a <strong>bitmap heap scan</strong>: collect every matching TID first, sort the TIDs by page, then read each table page once. Several of the 360 rows share a page, so this saves repeat visits. The price is the index order, which the page-by-page read discards. The next section shows when that choice wins.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="selectivity">
   <h2>Selectivity</h2>
   <p><strong>Selectivity</strong> is the fraction of a table’s rows that a condition matches. A condition matching 60 rows out of 60,000 is very selective; one matching half the table is not. The planner estimates it from statistics that the <code>ANALYZE</code> command collects, then picks the cheapest of three ways to fetch the rows:</p>
   <ul>
    <li>An <strong>index scan</strong> follows each entry to its row, in index order.</li>
    <li>A <strong>bitmap heap scan</strong> collects the TIDs, then reads each table page once, in page order.</li>
    <li>A <strong>sequential scan</strong> ignores the index and reads every page in order.</li>
   </ul>
   <p>Figure 5 widens a range one customer at a time. Watch how quickly the matching rows spread across the pages, and what that does to the three bars.</p>
   <SelectivityFigure number={5} />
   <p>By the third step, with 8 rows or 22% of the table matched, every page held a match. From there the index can only add work. A bitmap heap scan reads all six pages anyway, and an index scan reads some of them more than once. The turning point is page coverage, not a percentage: once every page holds a match, the index cannot save a read.</p>
   <p>The order of reads matters too. A sequential scan reads pages one after another, which storage and the operating system serve quickly. Index lookups jump around. PostgreSQL’s default cost settings charge a jumping read, <code>random_page_cost</code>, four times a sequential one, <code>seq_page_cost</code>. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-query.html#GUC-RANDOM-PAGE-COST">documentation</a> suggests lowering the first on SSDs or when the data is mostly in memory.</p>
   <p>On the generated table, with only the customer index, the planner kept using it until half the table matched.</p>
   <Sql code={wideRange} label="Fetch every order for customers up to 500" />
   <p>PostgreSQL used a bitmap heap scan up to <code>customer_id &lt;= 500</code>, which is half the table. From 550 it switched to a sequential scan. The reason is the generator: it hands out customer IDs in a repeating cycle, so each table page holds a run of about 35 consecutive customers. Customers 1 to 500 therefore sit on 922 of the 1,725 pages. If each customer’s orders were scattered evenly, the switch would come much sooner.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="composite">
   <h2>Composite indexes</h2>
   <p>A <strong>composite index</strong> has more than one key column. Its entries sort by the first column, then by the second among entries that tie on the first, and so on. The column order decides which entries sit together, and so which queries the index can answer by reading one stretch of entries.</p>
   <p>The query in figure 6 asks for customer 42’s two newest orders since 10 January. Switch between the three indexes and count the entries each one reads.</p>
   <CompositeFigure number={6} />
   <p>With <code>customer_id</code> alone, the index found all four of customer 42’s orders but knew nothing about their dates. Every row had to be fetched, checked and sorted. With <code>(customer_id, placed_at DESC)</code>, customer 42’s entries were already newest first. The answer was the first two entries of the run, and the scan stopped there. With the date first, every customer’s entries were interleaved by date, and the scan read 16 to find two.</p>
   <p>The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-multicolumn.html">PostgreSQL documentation</a> states the rule. Equality conditions on the leading columns, plus a range condition on the next column, limit how much of the index is read. Conditions on later columns are still checked inside the index, but they usually do not shorten the scan. So put the columns compared with <code>=</code> first, then the column used for the range or the sort.</p>
   <p>Here is the same history query for the generated table, asking for customer 42’s ten newest orders since 1 February:</p>
   <Sql code={history} label="Ask for customer 42’s ten newest orders since 1 February" />
   <p>Replace the customer index with a composite one and run it again.</p>
   <Sql code={compositeIndex} label="Replace the customer index with a composite index" />
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-labelledby="history-caption"><table><caption id="history-caption">The history query on the generated table with each index in place</caption><thead><tr><th>Index</th><th>Plan</th><th>Pages read</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>None</td><td>Sequential scan, then sort</td><td>1,725</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>(customer_id)</code></td><td>Bitmap heap scan: 60 rows fetched, 45 rejected, then sort</td><td>62</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>(customer_id, placed_at DESC, order_id DESC)</code></td><td>Index scan, stops after 10 rows, no sort</td><td>13</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>(placed_at DESC, customer_id)</code></td><td>Index scan through about 11,000 entries, then a partial sort</td><td>57</td></tr>
   </tbody></table></div>
   <p>The composite index read 13 pages: three to descend the tree and ten table pages, one per returned row. The reversed index found the right ten rows too, but only after reading every customer’s orders since 1 February, newest first.</p>
   <p>The <code>order_id DESC</code> column is a tiebreaker. Two orders placed in the same instant would otherwise have no defined order, and the query’s <code>ORDER BY</code> asks for one. Keep the <code>ORDER BY</code> in the query even when an index supplies the order, because SQL guarantees no order without it.</p>
   <p>B-trees can be read backwards, so an all-ascending index on <code>(customer_id, placed_at, order_id)</code> serves this query equally well. Mixed directions are different. A query sorting by <code>placed_at DESC, order_id ASC</code> needs an index declared that way, or its mirror image <code>(placed_at ASC, order_id DESC)</code>, because a backward scan reverses every column at once.</p>
   <p>A query with no condition on the leading column gets much less help. Here is the newest ten orders across all customers:</p>
   <Sql code={newest} label="Ask for the ten newest orders across all customers" />
   <p>The composite index offers no starting point for this query, because its entries are grouped by customer first. The newest orders are spread across 1,000 separate runs. PostgreSQL read all 1,725 table pages instead, then sorted the 961 matching rows to find the newest ten. PostgreSQL 18 added <strong>skip scan</strong>, which runs one index search for each distinct value of the leading column. It pays when that column has few distinct values, and 1,000 customers is not few.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="covering">
   <h2>Covering indexes and index-only scans</h2>
   <p>An <strong>index-only scan</strong> answers a query from the index without visiting the table. It is possible when every column the query uses is in the index. It skips the table only for pages known to be visible to all transactions.</p>
   <p>The first condition is about columns. The history query returns <code>total</code>, which the composite index lacks, so each of its ten rows needs a table visit. <code>INCLUDE</code> adds columns to every entry as stored values that are not part of the sort. An index holding every column a query needs is a <strong>covering index</strong> for that query.</p>
   <p>The second condition comes from how PostgreSQL handles concurrent changes. An update writes a new version of the row and keeps the old one for transactions that started earlier. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/storage/">storage article</a> follows those versions. Index entries carry no visibility information, so an entry alone cannot say whether this transaction should see its row. PostgreSQL therefore keeps a <strong>visibility map</strong>: one bit per table page, set when every row on the page is visible to all transactions. <code>VACUUM</code> sets the bits, and any change to a page clears its bit.</p>
   <p>Figure 7 shows one leaf page of the composite index beside the table. Try all four combinations of the two switches, and watch the heap-fetches counter and the visibility box beside each page.</p>
   <CoveringFigure number={7} />
   <p>Only one combination left the table alone: <code>total</code> included and the table vacuumed. Without the visibility bits, an index-only scan still visits each row’s page to check it.</p>
   <p>The generated table behaves the same way. Replace the composite index with a covering one.</p>
   <Sql code={coveringIndex} label="Replace the composite index with one that includes total" />
   <p>The plan becomes an <code>Index Only Scan</code>, but it reports <code>Heap Fetches: 10</code> and 13 pages read, as before. The table was just loaded, so no page is marked all-visible yet. Run <code>VACUUM</code> on its own, outside a transaction block:</p>
   <Sql code="VACUUM (ANALYZE) index_orders;" label="Vacuum the table to set its visibility bits" />
   <p>Run the history query again. It now reports <code>Heap Fetches: 0</code> and reads 4 pages.</p>
   <p>Covering has costs. The covering index is 2.9 MiB beside a 13.5 MiB table. <code>total</code> adds only a few bytes per entry, but a wide column would copy a lot of data into the index. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-index-only-scans.html">documentation</a> warns against wide included columns.</p>
   <p>It also notes that a table changing quickly gains little, because its pages keep losing their visibility bits. And an included column is still part of the index, so changing <code>total</code> now means changing the index too.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="writes">
   <h2>Index maintenance</h2>
   <p><strong>Index maintenance</strong> is the work of keeping every index in step with its table. Each index is another copy of some of the table’s data, so a write to the table is also a write to its indexes.</p>
   <p>Figure 8 follows one insert and one update through a table with up to four indexes. Change the number of indexes and count the pages each statement changes.</p>
   <WriteFigure number={8} />
   <p>With four indexes, the insert changed 7 pages, three of them for the split; with only the primary key, it changed 2. The update changed 1 page until an index held <code>total</code>, then 5.</p>
   <p>An insert adds an entry to every index. When the leaf page where an entry belongs is full, PostgreSQL first tries to make room by removing dead entries or merging duplicates. If that fails, the page <strong>splits</strong>: some of its entries move to a new page, and the parent page gains a pointer to it. A split can travel upwards when the parent is full too, and a split of the root adds a level to the tree.</p>
   <p>The update can be much cheaper. PostgreSQL writes a new row version for every update. If no indexed column changed and the new version fits on the same page, the update is a <strong>heap-only tuple</strong> (HOT) update. The existing index entries still lead to the row, and no B-tree index is touched. Change any column that any index holds, including an <code>INCLUDE</code> column, and every index on the table needs a new entry. In the <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/btree.html#BTREE-DELETION">B-tree documentation</a>’s words, such a change “always necessitates a new set of index tuples”.</p>
   <p>Both effects showed up on ordinary tables in PGlite, a WebAssembly build of PostgreSQL 18:</p>
   <ul>
    <li>Inserting 10,000 orders wrote 3.3 MB of write-ahead log, the record PostgreSQL keeps for crash recovery, with just the primary key. With four more indexes, the same insert wrote 7.7 MB.</li>
    <li>Updating <code>total</code> on 600 rows gave 600 HOT updates when no index held <code>total</code>, and none once the covering index included it. That table was created with <code>fillfactor = 90</code>, which leaves a tenth of each page free for new row versions.</li>
   </ul>
   <p>Free space on the page mattered too. With the default fillfactor of 100, the freshly loaded pages had no room for a new version, and none of the 600 updates was HOT even with <code>total</code> unindexed.</p>
   <p>Creating an index costs something too. A plain <code>CREATE INDEX</code> blocks writes to the table until it finishes. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-createindex.html#SQL-CREATEINDEX-CONCURRENTLY"><code>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY</code></a> allows writes during the build but takes longer, and a failed build leaves an invalid index behind, to be dropped or rebuilt with <code>REINDEX INDEX CONCURRENTLY</code>.</p>
   <p>An index that no query reads still costs every write. The <code>pg_stat_user_indexes</code> view counts the scans of each index:</p>
   <Sql code={usage} label="Count how often each index on a table has been scanned" />
   <p>An index whose <code>idx_scan</code> stays at zero over a representative period is a candidate for removal. First check whether it enforces a primary key or unique constraint, which needs the index whatever the count says. A <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/indexes-partial.html"><strong>partial index</strong></a> cuts the cost from the other side. It indexes only rows matching its own <code>WHERE</code> clause, such as pending orders, so it stays small and inserts of other rows skip it.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="check">
   <h2>Checking an index with EXPLAIN</h2>
   <p><code>EXPLAIN</code> shows the plan PostgreSQL chose for a query. <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> also runs the query and reports what each step actually did. Since PostgreSQL 18 it reports the pages each step touched as well; on older versions, add <code>BUFFERS</code>. Here <code>ANALYZE</code> means run and measure. The separate <code>ANALYZE</code> command collects the statistics the planner uses.</p>
   <Sql code={explain(history)} label="Run the history query and report its plan" />
   <p>With the composite index in place, the important part of the plan looks like this, simplified: costs, page counts, type casts and PostgreSQL 18’s extra detail lines are left out.</p>
   <pre class="plan"><code>{plan}</code></pre>
   <p>Read it from the most indented line outwards. The index scan produces rows in order, and <code>Limit</code> stops asking for more after ten. Three parts of a plan tell you what an index did:</p>
   <ul>
    <li><strong>The node type.</strong> <code>Seq Scan</code>, <code>Index Scan</code>, <code>Index Only Scan</code> or <code>Bitmap Heap Scan</code> names the route.</li>
    <li><strong><code>Index Cond</code> against <code>Filter</code>.</strong> Conditions under <code>Index Cond</code> were used in the index search. Conditions under <code>Filter</code> were checked on rows after fetching them, and <code>Rows Removed by Filter</code> counts the wasted fetches.</li>
    <li><strong>Buffers.</strong> <code>shared hit</code> and <code>read</code> count pages. A temporary table reports them as <code>local</code>. Compare them across indexes, as the tables above do.</li>
   </ul>
   <p>A paid-only version of the history query produces a <code>Filter</code>:</p>
   <Sql code={residual} label="Ask for customer 42’s ten newest paid orders" />
   <p>The index has no <code>status</code>, so PostgreSQL fetched 12 rows and discarded 2 pending ones to return 10. Two wasted fetches is cheap. For a customer whose recent orders were mostly pending, the scan would read much further. If that filter matters, an index on <code>(customer_id, status, placed_at DESC)</code> moves it into the <code>Index Cond</code>, at the cost of splitting each customer’s history by status.</p>
   <p>The <a href={experimentUrl} download="index-experiment.sql">complete experiment</a> runs every step in this article in order, with a plan after each. Run it in a fresh session with <code>psql -f index-experiment.sql</code>, or statement by statement in a query editor with autocommit on. The numbers here come from PGlite 0.5.8, which reports PostgreSQL 18.3. A server may report slightly different page counts, and none of the numbers are timings.</p>
   <p>Before relying on an index, test it with realistic data, the application’s real queries, and its real mix of reads and writes.</p>
   <p class="example-note">The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=index-order">index-order lesson</a> runs the history experiment in your browser, and the <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=index-selectivity">index-selectivity lesson</a> widens a filter until the planner gives up on the index.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="series-reading" aria-labelledby="continue-title"><h2 id="continue-title">Further reading</h2>
